[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {Black now has to face The London. You'll face it 15% of the time (it's played in almost 33.5 million games). There are many ways of playing against the London, but for the purposes of this course we are going to challenge the central white pawn with 2...c5.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {Taking is also very logical. It's played 8% of the time. But we're regaining the pawn with Na6.} 3...Na6 4.Qd4 {Trying to defend doesn't work. We have a check on a5.} 4...Qa5+ 5.Nc3 {Play can continue with developing a piece and blocking the check. We'll regain the pawn.} 5...Nxc5 6.b4 {This move is a fork, but we can deliver a fork of our own. The crafty Ne6 fits the bill.} 6...Ne6 7.Qc4 {White sidesteps. We'll just retreat the queen, leaving the f4-bishop under attack.} 7...Qd8 8.Be5 {It moves forward.} 8...d6 {But we can still bully it. Our pieces are quite well placed. Our bishop is coming to d7 and if white takes on f6, we'll recapture with our g-pawn, opening the file for our rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky is also another possible early deviation. It's only played 4% of the time, since there are so many second moves possible. That's still almost 8.5 million games! We'll prepare our kingside fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Bxf6 {The bishop trades itself off, damaging our structure slightly. Our f-pawns are doubled.} 3...exf6 4.e3 {White places pawns on dark squares and supports d4. We'll place our d-pawn in the center.} 4...d5 5.c4 {White undermines our center, but trading is our plan.} 5...dxc4 6.Bxc4 {White recaptures. d6 is a good, active location for our dark-squared bishop.} 6...Bd6 7.Nc3 {Developing the knight. We'll get our knight out as well. We have a plan of Nd7, f5, Nf6, bringing it to a location where it will have a strong influence upon the center.} 7...Nd7 8.Bb3 {The bishop retreats. We'll continue our plan to exert control over e4.} 8...f5 9.Nf3 {White develops. And we'll complete the knight maneuver.} 9...Nf6 10.Qc2 {The queen is developed.} 10...O-O {And we castle. It seems our knight will have a good home on e4 after it gains the support of the rook. It might be useful to push ...c6 and prevent White from accessing the d5-square.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {Black now has to face The London. You'll face it 15% of the time (it's played in almost 33.5 million games). There are many ways of playing against the London, but for the purposes of this course we are going to challenge the central white pawn with 2...c5.} 2...c5 3.dxc5 {Taking is also very logical. It's played 8% of the time. But we're regaining the pawn with Na6.} 3...Na6 4.f3 {White wants a big center. We'll take on c5.} 4...Nxc5 5.e4 {e4 comes. We'll stake a claim to the center by playing d6.} 5...d6 6.Nc3 {White develops the knight. Now a6 is a good multi-purpose move, preventing any white minor piece from accessing b5, and preparing for queenside expansion and fianchettoing the c8-bishop.} 6...a6 7.Qd2 {The queen develops and prepares castling queenside. b5 is very natural.} 7...b5 8.O-O-O {White castles. We'll fianchetto the bishop.} 8...Bb7 9.Nge2 {White develops their king's knight.} 9...g6 {And now we prepare the double fianchetto. All in all, it seems our pieces will have pretty good squares, and White will be taken out of their London System comfort zone, since they won't face a traditional queen's pawn opening setup. We can castle short and get control of the c-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky is also another possible early deviation. It's only played 4% of the time, since there are so many second moves possible. That's still almost 8.5 million games! We'll prepare our kingside fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Nf3 {White continues with normal development 8% of the time. We'll fianchetto.} 3...Bg7 4.Nbd2 {White develops another knight. We'll play d5 and prevent White from playing e4.} 4...d5 5.e3 {White settles for e3. We'll castle.} 5...O-O 6.Bd3 {Developing the bishop. We'll strike at the white center.} 6...c5 7.c3 {Defending. We'll get ready to bring out our queen's bishop. The idea is to exchange off the good bishop on d3.} 7...b6 8.O-O {White castles. Now we can continue with our plan. The d3-bishop is pinned against the rook.} 8...Ba6 9.Bxa6 {Trading. Of course, we recapture.} 9...Nxa6 10.Ne5 {The knight arrives to a good square: e5. Our knight is slightly offside, so we'll bring it back towards the center.} 10...Nc7 11.a4 {a4 covers the b5 square. We'll attack the bishop.} 11...Ne6 12.Bh4 {The bishop retreats.} 12...Qc7 {And finally we connect rooks. Our play could continue with opening the c-file, or perhaps offering a trade of knights on e4, depending on how White wants to continue!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {Black now has to face The London. You'll face it 15% of the time (it's played in almost 33.5 million games). There are many ways of playing against the London, but for the purposes of this course we are going to challenge the central white pawn with 2...c5.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White defends the pawn and plays e3 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain. This is the most popular move, played 54% of the time. We'll prepare to fianchetto.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White continues developing the pieces normally. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 4...Bg7 5.c3 {White continues with their London pawn structure. We'll prepare a second fianchetto.} 5...b6 6.Nbd2 {White develops the knight. We'll fianchetto.} 6...Bb7 7.Bd3 {The bishop comes out to d3. And we'll get castled.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles. White usually wants to invade the e5-square so it is a good idea to defend it now.} 8...d6 9.h3 {White gives the bishop a retreat square. We'll bring our knight out and support a potential pawn break with ...e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.a4 {White expands on the queenside. The pawn does well to control b5. We'll take on d4.} 10...cxd4 11.exd4 {White opens up the e-file a bit more for the rook. But here, keep an eye out: sometimes e5 is tactically warranted!} 11...e5 12.dxe5 {White can't win a pawn because of the undefended d3-bishop. We recapture, opening the d-file.} 12...dxe5 13.Bxe5 {This is just an example. We'll exchange on e5.} 13...Nxe5 14.Nxe5 {White recaptures, defending the bishop. Now we can play ...Nh5, when the white minor pieces are suddenly rather loose.} 14...Nh5 15.Ndf3 {A second knight joins the defensive chain. Our g7-bishop is good here, but we'll take on e5 for a very specific tactical reason.} 15...Bxe5 16.Nxe5 {White recaptures.} 16...Nf4 {And we can jump to f4, attacking the bishop and the pawn on g2. We also have dangerous ideas of Qg5.} *

