[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {Black now has to face The London. You'll face it 15% of the time (it's played in almost 33.5 million games). There are many ways of playing against the London, but for the purposes of this course we are going to challenge the central white pawn with 2...c5.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White defends the pawn and plays e3 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain. This is the most popular move, played 54% of the time. We'll prepare to fianchetto.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White continues developing the pieces normally. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 4...Bg7 5.c3 {White continues with their London pawn structure. We'll prepare a second fianchetto.} 5...b6 6.Nbd2 {White develops the knight. We'll fianchetto.} 6...Bb7 7.Bd3 {The bishop comes out to d3. And we'll get castled.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles. White usually wants to invade the e5-square so it is a good idea to defend it now.} 8...d6 9.h3 {White gives the bishop a retreat square. We'll bring our knight out and support a potential pawn break with ...e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.a4 {White expands on the queenside. The pawn does well to control b5. We'll take on d4.} 10...cxd4 11.exd4 {White opens up the e-file a bit more for the rook. But here, keep an eye out: sometimes e5 is tactically warranted!} 11...e5 12.Bg5 {White pins us.} 12...Qc7 {But we step out of the way and our queen is developed, our rooks are connected, and e5 is on the board!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {Black now has to face The London. You'll face it 15% of the time (it's played in almost 33.5 million games). There are many ways of playing against the London, but for the purposes of this course we are going to challenge the central white pawn with 2...c5.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White defends the pawn and plays e3 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain. This is the most popular move, played 54% of the time. We'll prepare to fianchetto.} 3...g6 4.Nf3 {White continues developing the pieces normally. We'll complete the fianchetto.} 4...Bg7 5.c3 {White continues with their London pawn structure. We'll prepare a second fianchetto.} 5...b6 6.Nbd2 {White develops the knight. We'll fianchetto.} 6...Bb7 7.Bd3 {The bishop comes out to d3. And we'll get castled.} 7...O-O 8.O-O {White castles. White usually wants to invade the e5-square so it is a good idea to defend it now.} 8...d6 9.h3 {White gives the bishop a retreat square. We'll bring our knight out and support a potential pawn break with ...e5.} 9...Nbd7 10.Re1 {The rook enters the e-file, perhaps in preparation for pushing the e-pawn. We can opt for a similar plan.} 10...Re8 11.e4 {White pushes in the center as planned. And we'll strike back at the white center with ...e5.} 11...e5 12.dxe5 {White takes. We can recapture and stay active.} 12...Nxe5 13.Bxe5 {White trades off the strong knight. We'll take back, and the d3-bishop is momentarily under attack from our queen.} 13...dxe5 14.Qe2 {White defends the bishop.} 14...a6 {The middle game will largely consist of queenside play, now that the center is practically closed with two e-pawns facing each other.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bg5 {The Trompowsky is also another possible early deviation. It's only played 4% of the time, since there are so many second moves possible. That's still almost 8.5 million games! We'll prepare our kingside fianchetto.} 2...g6 3.Bxf6 {The bishop trades itself off, damaging our structure slightly. Our f-pawns are doubled.} 3...exf6 4.e3 {White places pawns on dark squares and supports d4. We'll place our d-pawn in the center.} 4...d5 5.g3 {White prepares to fianchetto 13% of the time here. Because of the strong central white pawn chain, we are now going to reposition our bishop on d6, encouraged by the idea of a future h4 targeting the white kingside.} 5...Bd6 6.Bg2 {White develops and attacks the d-pawn, which we'll support with c6.} 6...c6 7.Nd2 {White develops the b1-knight. We'll bring out our queen's bishop, in preparation for completing our queenside development.} 7...Be6 8.Ne2 {White develops the other knight. Let's gain some space and expand on the kingside.} 8...h5 9.h4 {White prevents us from pushing forward. We'll develop our last minor piece.} 9...Nd7 10.Rc1 {It seems White really wants c4. We'll inhibit it with Nb6.} 10...Nb6 11.b3 {White wants to go c4. But in preparing it with b3, we now have induced weakened dark squares that we'll be able to infiltrate with our bishop! First, let's get our queen out.} 11...Qe7 12.O-O {White castles, in preparation for the forthcoming middle-game battle. We'll disrupt any intended c4 advance by kicking the c1-rook.} 12...Ba3 13.Rb1 {It moves to the side.} 13...Bf5 {And finally this move completely prevents the plan of c4, which White had been preparing. We'll be castling queenside next and going g5, with the added benefit of having neutralized White's counterplay! The rook on b1 looks misplaced.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 1: White Plays London or Trompowsky / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/2b340098-a072-406d-85f2-3bdeeabfa83e/lines"]

1.d4 {The Budapest Gambit is a great choice if you want to surprise your opponent very early in the game and get a fighting position. Firstly, let's take a look at what happens if White goes for the Trompowsky or the London, as this poses a change for our setup.} 1...Nf6 2.Bf4 {Black now has to face The London. You'll face it 15% of the time (it's played in almost 33.5 million games). There are many ways of playing against the London, but for the purposes of this course we are going to challenge the central white pawn with 2...c5.} 2...c5 3.e3 {White defends the pawn and plays e3 now that the bishop is out of the pawn chain. This is the most popular move, played 54% of the time. We'll prepare to fianchetto.} 3...g6 4.dxc5 {This option is always present for White. The f1-bishop already covers the a6 square, so the knight can't come out to a6 in this variation. In this position, White takes only 3% of the time.} 4...Qa5+ {We'll regain the pawn with a check, though.} *

