[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e3 {White opens up an attack on our knight only 4% of the time, but it makes sense as a move. We take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.Nh3 {The knight is headed to f4. We should insist on our gambit play here, with d5!} 5...d5 6.Qxd5 {White grabs the pawn with the queen. Naturally, we don't exchange queens.} 6...Qf6 7.Nf4 {The knight is transferred to f4. We'll attack the queen and force it away from the center.} 7...c6 8.Qd1 {White retreats to d1. While playing a gambit, developing is key.} 8...Bf5 9.Be2 {White prepares castling. We continue to develop our minor pieces.} 9...Nbd7 10.Nc3 {Developing the knight. We'll castle queenside, setting up potential discoveries on the d-file against the queen.} 10...O-O-O 11.O-O {White castles. They needed to get their king into safety but now we'll attack the queen.} 11...Nc5 12.Qe1 {The queen moves away.} 12...h5 {And we attack on the kingside! White is in a precarious position!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {Nc3 does nothing about the tension between the pawns, but develops a knight. It's chosen 17% of the time. We'll capture on d4.} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures. We can develop and attack the white queen.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {The queen retreats. We'll bring our bishop out and pin the knight.} 5...Bb4 6.Bg5 {White elects to pin us, too. We'll take on c3 and damage their pawn structure.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {Recapturing. We'll take steps to break the pin on our knight.} 7...h6 8.Bxf6 {The bishop takes our knight. We'll get our queen out, hitting c3.} 8...Qxf6 9.Qc2 {The queen defends. Next, we'll make room to get our bishop out.} 9...d6 10.e3 {White opens up their bishop. We'll attack the white queen.} 10...Bf5 11.Bd3 {If the bishop blocks, trading is the right idea here.} 11...Bxd3 12.Qxd3 {The queen recaptures.} 12...Ne5 {And we bring our knight to the center, with a better position and the superior pawn structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.e3 {e3 supports d4. Expect to see this move 7% of the time. We should trade and develop with a tempo yet again.} 3...exd4 4.exd4 {White recaptures. We'll check the white king.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Nc3 {White usually blocks with the knight here. Let's continue actively in the center.} 5...d5 6.Nf3 {Play can continue with Nf3. We'll castle.} 6...O-O 7.Bd3 {White develops the light-squared bishop. Now we can take on c4, and the bishop has to lose a tempo.} 7...dxc4 8.Bxc4 {The bishop recaptures. We'll continue our development.} 8...Nc6 9.O-O {White castles. Let's put pressure on d4.} 9...Bg4 10.Be3 {The bishop supports d4.} 10...Qd6 {And we connect rooks, getting ready to bring them to the center and keep adding pressure to the d4-pawn.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.d5 {White simply advances the pawn 18% of the time. Developing is the answer.} 3...Bb4+ 4.Bd2 {White can block and propose a trade. We can support our bishop here favorably. If White takes, our rook will be unleashed on the a-file.} 4...a5 5.a3 {White forces matters. We'll capture.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Nxd2 {The knight recaptures. We'll bring our b8-knight out, eyeing the useful square c5.} 6...Na6 7.e4 {White releases their bishop. And our knight comes to c5, attacking the e-pawn.} 7...Nc5 8.Qc2 {White defends the e4-pawn. We'll prepare to get the bishop out.} 8...d6 9.Ne2 {Ne2 develops a knight.} 9...O-O {And we can get castled. We have two really well placed knights in the resulting position, and we can even dream about jumping to h5 into f4 at some point, or even pushing f5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 13"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.Nc3 {Nc3 does nothing about the tension between the pawns, but develops a knight. It's chosen 17% of the time. We'll capture on d4.} 3...exd4 4.Qxd4 {White recaptures. We can develop and attack the white queen.} 4...Nc6 5.Qd1 {The queen retreats. We'll bring our bishop out and pin the knight.} 5...Bb4 6.Bd2 {The bishop breaks the pin.} 6...O-O {Here we can simply castle.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e3 {White opens up an attack on our knight only 4% of the time, but it makes sense as a move. We take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.f4 {Attacking our knight in the middle. This move is actually played 20% of the time. People just love attacking knights in the center with pawns. Let's retreat, but still allow for the g6 and fianchetto plan.} 5...Nec6 6.Nf3 {White continues with development. We'll prepare a pathway to get our queenside pieces out.} 6...d6 7.Bd3 {White develops their bishop, facing our kingside. We'll bring out our b8-knight. It can come to c5 and pester the white bishop.} 7...Nd7 8.O-O {White castles. We'll push the bishop back.} 8...Nc5 9.Bc2 {White wants to keep their bishop. Let's restrict the white queenside.} 9...a5 10.Nc3 {The knight is developed. We'll fianchetto.} 10...g6 11.e4 {Pushing the pawns and expanding in the center.} 11...Bg7 {The bishop is fianchettoed. We'll castle, develop the bishop, and our pieces have active squares.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e3 {White opens up an attack on our knight only 4% of the time, but it makes sense as a move. We take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.Nh3 {The knight is headed to f4. We should insist on our gambit play here, with d5!} 5...d5 6.cxd5 {The pawn captures. This is actually the most common move, played 37% of the time. We can now split the White pawn structure on the kingside. While this involves giving up the bishop pair, it means that when White castles kingside, the king will be more exposed than normal.} 6...Bxh3 7.gxh3 {White recaptures. We keep developing.} 7...Bd6 8.Nc3 {White supports their center. We'll castle.} 8...O-O 9.Bg2 {The bishop is placed on the diagonal supporting d5. We continue to play actively.} 9...Nbd7 10.O-O {White castles. Now that White has committed the king, we should take measures to occupy vulnerable squares in front of the castled position.} 10...Ng6 11.f4 {Play can continue with f4. We'll play f5, preventing White from expanding in the center with e4, and fixing the backward e-pawn.} 11...f5 12.Kh1 {The king clears a square for the rook. Let's bring our knight to a dangerous outpost.} 12...Nh4 13.Rg1 {The rook is placed opposite our king. We connect our rooks.} 13...Qe7 14.Bd2 {The bishop develops.} 14...Rae8 {And we bring another rook to the center. White's pawn on e3 is definitely a target, and we have great control over the e4-square, providing us with some central space.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e4 {White opens up an attack on our knight and places another pawn in the center. 4.e4 is only played 6% of the time, but has some merit to it. We'll take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.f4 {Expanding and chasing our knight away.} 5...Nec6 6.Nf3 {The knight is developed to f3. The difference is that our bishop will occupy the c5-square as opposed to the b4-square.} 6...Bc5 7.Bd3 {The bishop is developed to d3. We'll play d6, preparing to get the other bishop out.} 7...d6 8.Nc3 {Developing. We castle.} 8...O-O 9.a3 {White prepares b4. So we clamp down on this idea by playing a5.} 9...a5 10.Qe2 {The queen is developed, perhaps supporting a Be3 move to trade our strong bishop. We'll bring our bishop out, pinning the knight and threatening the disruptive Nd4.} 10...Bg4 11.Be3 {Proposing a trade. Let's make it harder for White to trade off our strong bishop and centralize our knight.} 11...Nd4 12.Qf2 {The only move for White is now Qf2 to save the queen and keep the minor pieces defended. We'll take on f3 with the bishop.} 12...Bxf3 13.gxf3 {After the capture on f3, White's structure is compromised.} 13...Nb3 {But more importantly, we'll likely have a dark-squared bishop trade and we'll control the c5-square to put a knight there. We have a dynamic position.} *

