[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e4 {White opens up an attack on our knight and places another pawn in the center. 4.e4 is only played 6% of the time, but has some merit to it. We'll take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.f4 {Expanding and chasing our knight away.} 5...Nec6 6.Be3 {White develops the bishop. We will develop as well, to the usual square.} 6...Bb4+ 7.Nc3 {White blocks with the knight. We'll capture, doubling White's pawns.} 7...Bxc3+ 8.bxc3 {White recaptures. Let's check the white king.} 8...Qh4+ 9.g3 {White blocks the check.} 9...Qe7 {We retreat and attack e4. Our knight will develop to a6 soon.} *

