1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e3 {White opens up an attack on our knight only 4% of the time, but it makes sense as a move. We take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.Nc3 {White can also develop to c3. We'll fianchetto.} 5...g6 6.Nh3 {The knight is developed to h3. Our bishop comes out.} 6...Bg7 7.Nf4 {On f4 the knight has a good home. Let's castle.} 7...O-O 8.Be2 {White prepares castling.} 8...d6 {And we support our knight on e5. Also, our other knight is coming to c5.} *

