1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White plays the conventional capture. It's by far the most common move. You'll see it 52% of the time, which is more than 4.5 million games! We'll bring our knight to g4, where it attacks the e5-pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.e4 {White opens up an attack on our knight and places another pawn in the center. 4.e4 is only played 6% of the time, but has some merit to it. We'll take on e5.} 4...Nxe5 5.f4 {Expanding and chasing our knight away.} 5...Nec6 6.Be3 {White develops the bishop. We will develop as well, to the usual square.} 6...Bb4+ 7.Nd2 {The knight blocks on d2. We'll prepare to activate our queenside.} 7...d6 8.Nf3 {White develops another knight.} 8...O-O {Now we get castled. Our pieces won't have any problems getting developed and our king got castled faster than White's.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.e3 {e3 supports d4. Expect to see this move 7% of the time. We should trade and develop with a tempo yet again.} 3...exd4 4.exd4 {White recaptures. We'll check the white king.} 4...Bb4+ 5.Bd2 {The bishop blocks and proposes a trade. At this point, players choose this move 47% of the time. We'll exchange.} 5...Bxd2+ 6.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures. We'll push in the center.} 6...d5 7.Nc3 {If White continues with Nc3...} 7...O-O {We just castle, with a slightly more comfortable position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Avoids The Budapest Gambit / Study 3: White's Other Options / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/e61e13ac-31f0-4a6d-828f-f481f23cd779/studies/9d8589ce-8cc2-4f2f-80b3-c017f61360a9/lines"]

1.d4 {You've reached the third study of the Budapest Gambit. In this study, White will actually allow us to enter the first moves of the gambit! However, they'll start deviating on move 3. We'll explore a conventional-looking 3. dxe5, but without the logical continuations of Nf3 and Bf4.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds another pawn to the center. We offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.d5 {White simply advances the pawn 18% of the time. Developing is the answer.} 3...Bb4+ 4.Nc3 {Blocking with the knight isn't so good. Let's exchange and double the white pawns.} 4...Bxc3+ 5.bxc3 {The pawn recaptures.} 5...d6 {And we're left with a better structure.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {White just develops the bishop 23% of the time. There is no way to defend the e-pawn. We can simply castle, as we don't need to rush to regain the pawn.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles, too. We'll bring our rook to the half-open e-file.} 7...Re8 8.Nc3 {The white knight is developed, but we have enough pieces attacking the e5-pawn and will just capture it.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Nxe5 {This is an option White may have. We recapture.} 9...Nxe5 10.b3 {The dark-squared bishop needs a place to go. It is always a good idea to grab some space on the queenside in this line of the Budapest.} 10...a5 11.Bb2 {White fianchettoes. An added benefit of ...a5 before any other pawn pushes is that we can reroute our rook to the kingside!} 11...Ra6 12.Qd5 {Attacking our bishop. Our bishop can slide back to a7.} 12...Ba7 13.Ne4 {The knight moves up, adding another attacker on our e5 knight. We don't have to defend it!} 13...Rh6 14.Bxe5 {Was the knight free? We can displace the queen from the defense of the bishop.} 14...c6 15.Bf6 {This is a crazy response, but quite possible. Now our pawns are damaged.} 15...gxf6 16.Qf5 {The queen stays on the kingside, applying pressure. We'll attack h2.} 16...Bb8 17.Ng3 {White blocks.} 17...d5 {And we've got d5, opening an attack on the queen and freeing up our position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {White just develops the bishop 23% of the time. There is no way to defend the e-pawn. We can simply castle, as we don't need to rush to regain the pawn.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles, too. We'll bring our rook to the half-open e-file.} 7...Re8 8.Nc3 {The white knight is developed, but we have enough pieces attacking the e5-pawn and will just capture it.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Nxe5 {This is an option White may have. We recapture.} 9...Nxe5 10.b3 {The dark-squared bishop needs a place to go. It is always a good idea to grab some space on the queenside in this line of the Budapest.} 10...a5 11.Bb2 {White fianchettoes. An added benefit of ...a5 before any other pawn pushes is that we can reroute our rook to the kingside!} 11...Ra6 12.Nd5 {The knight moves up to d5. But this allows us immediate kingside pressure.} 12...Rh6 13.Nf4 {The knight goes back to defend. Our queen comes in, threatening immediate mate!} 13...Qh4 14.h3 {White defends. And we play d6, consolidating our pieces. It also means we have a bishop sacrifice on h3 if the knight leaves f4!} 14...d6 {And we play d6, consolidating our pieces.} 15.Re1 {Simple moves don't work for White.} 15...Ng6 {This simple retreating move encourages an exchange, but if White takes it they'll be lost! Rxg6 will be too much pressure. White needs to tread carefully.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {White just develops the bishop 23% of the time. There is no way to defend the e-pawn. We can simply castle, as we don't need to rush to regain the pawn.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles, too. We'll bring our rook to the half-open e-file.} 7...Re8 8.Nc3 {The white knight is developed, but we have enough pieces attacking the e5-pawn and will just capture it.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Nxe5 {This is an option White may have. We recapture.} 9...Nxe5 10.b3 {The dark-squared bishop needs a place to go. It is always a good idea to grab some space on the queenside in this line of the Budapest.} 10...a5 11.Bb2 {White fianchettoes. An added benefit of ...a5 before any other pawn pushes is that we can reroute our rook to the kingside!} 11...Ra6 12.Qd5 {Attacking our bishop. Our bishop can slide back to a7.} 12...Ba7 13.Ne4 {The knight moves up, adding another attacker on our e5 knight. We don't have to defend it!} 13...Rh6 14.Qxa5 {Let's see what happens if White grabs the a5-pawn. Our bishop is attacked, so we'll move it to b6 and counterattack the white queen.} 14...Bb6 15.Qa8 {The queen moves down to a8, trying to do some damage. We'll prepare to get our queen's bishop out. We are not afraid of the white queen.} 15...d6 16.c5 {c5 seems clever, trying to wreck our structure. Should we ignore it? Let's threaten mate on h2 and, at the same time, the knight on e4!} 16...Qh4 17.Bxe5 {The bishop takes, temporarily defending h2. After recapturing, our threats will remain.} 17...dxe5 18.f3 {f3 defends the knight. We'll take on h2.} 18...Qxh2+ 19.Kf2 {White is totally lost. We give another check, further exposing the opponent's king.} 19...Qh4+ 20.g3 {White blocks. But now we return to h2.} 20...Qh2+ 21.Ke1 {The king is back to the square he began the game on.} 21...f5 {And we attack the piece that defends g3. This is an example of an attack you could get even deep in a main line of the Budapest Gambit, despite your opponent knowing the best ten moves.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {White just develops the bishop 23% of the time. There is no way to defend the e-pawn. We can simply castle, as we don't need to rush to regain the pawn.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles, too. We'll bring our rook to the half-open e-file.} 7...Re8 8.Nc3 {The white knight is developed, but we have enough pieces attacking the e5-pawn and will just capture it.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Nxe5 {This is an option White may have. We recapture.} 9...Nxe5 10.b3 {The dark-squared bishop needs a place to go. It is always a good idea to grab some space on the queenside in this line of the Budapest.} 10...a5 11.Bb2 {White fianchettoes. An added benefit of ...a5 before any other pawn pushes is that we can reroute our rook to the kingside!} 11...Ra6 12.Ne4 {Attacking our bishop with the knight. Our bishop retreats to safety.} 12...Ba7 13.Ng3 {The knight is transferred to the kingside. We bring our attacker across.} 13...Rh6 14.Nf5 {The knight can gain a tempo on our rook. But we can gain the tempo back on the knight.} 14...Rf6 15.f4 {A move like f4 opens up White too much on the kingside. We take the knight.} 15...Rxf5 16.fxe5 {White recaptures ours. They're left with isolated doubled e-pawns, and e3 is already loose. Now d6 begins to undermine the pawns. We also defend our f5-rook.} 16...d6 17.exd6 {White takes. We capture the rook.} 17...Rxf1+ 18.Kxf1 {The king recaptures.} 18...Bxe3 {And we capture a pawn in the center. Our bishop is a monster around the king!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {White just develops the bishop 23% of the time. There is no way to defend the e-pawn. We can simply castle, as we don't need to rush to regain the pawn.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles, too. We'll bring our rook to the half-open e-file.} 7...Re8 8.Nc3 {The white knight is developed, but we have enough pieces attacking the e5-pawn and will just capture it.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Nxe5 {This is an option White may have. We recapture.} 9...Nxe5 10.b3 {The dark-squared bishop needs a place to go. It is always a good idea to grab some space on the queenside in this line of the Budapest.} 10...a5 11.Bb2 {White fianchettoes. An added benefit of ...a5 before any other pawn pushes is that we can reroute our rook to the kingside!} 11...Ra6 12.Qd5 {Attacking our bishop. Our bishop can slide back to a7.} 12...Ba7 13.Ne4 {The knight moves up, adding another attacker on our e5 knight. We don't have to defend it!} 13...Rh6 14.Bxe5 {Was the knight free? We can displace the queen from the defense of the bishop.} 14...c6 15.Bf6 {This is a crazy response, but quite possible. Now our pawns are damaged.} 15...gxf6 16.Qd3 {The queen retreats. Moves like d5 are possible. White cannot take.} 16...d5 17.cxd5 {This reply doesn't spot our threat. In this middle game, we have some tricks!} 17...Bf5 {As we're winning a piece if this happens.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Qd5 {White could try this move, attacking the bishop on c5 and defending e5 for one more turn. They will try it 15% of the time. However, after Qe7 we deal with both ideas. We're going to be taking on e5 soon.} 6...Qe7 7.Nc3 {Nc3 is now the most popular move, with the simple idea of development. But now we can take the e5-pawn and have ideas of ...c6 to kick the queen out.} 7...Ncxe5 8.Nxe5 {You'll most likely see a trade here. But we just bring our other knight back into the center.} 8...Nxe5 9.Be2 {White may prepare castling. But now we can make use of the ...c6 move which also controls the d5- and b5-squares. The c3-knight now doesn't have access to these squares.} 9...c6 10.Qe4 {Qd1 was also popular, but it really poses no problems. Against Qd1, we can go for normal development. But against Qe4, there's a nice idea I want you to remember: ...d6 to play ...f5.} 10...d6 11.O-O {White castles...} 11...a6 {And we're playing ...a6 before ...f5. We want to have a safe square for our dark-squared bishop. We'll play ...f5 and ...0-0 in the next few moves. White won't have any advantage at all. In fact, they'll have a problem piece: the dark-squared bishop.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 1: White Plays 4.Nf3 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/320b80b9-8889-40ad-9685-90e99864d27b/lines"]

1.d4 {This is a study on one of the main lines of the Budapest Gambit: 4. Nf3. As you can see by the title of this move, White brings their knight out early. The purpose of this is to defend the pawn they have recently acquired by accepting our gambit. The games can continue with fairly equal evaluations, but Black always has very aggressive ideas that can result in powerful attacks!} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {White adds a second pawn to the central battle. We'll offer our gambit.} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {And so they take it! We'll play our knight to g4, attacking the e-pawn as before.} 3...Ng4 4.Nf3 {The knight defends the recent pawn acquisition. You'll come across 4.Nf3 by far the most often, with 54%. It's played in more than 2.1 million games. Let's develop and attack f2.} 4...Bc5 5.e3 {White blocks the attack. At the same time, it's good we provoked this move because the dark-squared bishop is trapped behind the pawns. We'll continue to go for rapid development, attacking the e5-pawn once more.} 5...Nc6 6.Be2 {White just develops the bishop 23% of the time. There is no way to defend the e-pawn. We can simply castle, as we don't need to rush to regain the pawn.} 6...O-O 7.O-O {White castles, too. We'll bring our rook to the half-open e-file.} 7...Re8 8.Nc3 {The white knight is developed, but we have enough pieces attacking the e5-pawn and will just capture it.} 8...Ngxe5 9.b3 {b3, supporting c4 and giving the c1-bishop a square to fianchetto is a good move. We trade a pair of knights.} 9...Nxf3+ 10.Bxf3 {White recaptures.} 10...Ne5 {We attack the bishop. This will force the bishop back. The position seems to have transposed back into the main line.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 {Blocking with the knight on c3 is also possible. And it's played half the time. We'll take on c3 and double the white pawns.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {They recapture. Our queen comes to e7 to put pressure on the weak e5-pawn.} 7...Qe7 8.Qd5 {White defends the attacked e5-pawn. It is impossible to add an attacker to it without giving up another pawn. This is completely fine, as getting active pieces is more important. We play in gambit style and push f6!} 8...f6 9.exf6 {White captures. Our knight returns to f6, attacking the white queen.} 9...Nxf6 10.Qd3 {The queen retreats, defending the two weak c-pawns. We'll prepare to get our bishop out.} 10...d6 11.g3 {White can continue with a fianchetto. Now castling is sensible.} 11...O-O 12.Bg2 {The bishop goes to g2. We'll double on the half-open e-file.} 12...Re8 13.O-O {White allows us to regain the pawn by capturing on e2. The queens are going to be exchanged. Let's see how this works out.} 13...Qxe2 14.Qxe2 {White recaptures. Our rook arrives on the seventh.} 14...Rxe2 15.Nd4 {And now White forces us to fix their structure. We have little choice but to take on d4.} 15...Nxd4 16.cxd4 {The pawn structure has been repaired, and White's pressure on the queenside is immense. Rather than defend passively, we offer our b7-pawn in order to get our remaining pieces out.} 16...Be6 17.Bxb7 {White grabs the pawn. We'll attack the bishop. The c4-pawn is also under fire.} 17...Rb8 18.Rab1 {White's rook defends. We'll take on a2.} 18...Rxa2 19.Rfe1 {White attacks our bishop. We'll take on c4. White had set up a nasty trap. The natural reply Kf7 would have lost to 20.Rxe6 Kxe6 21.Bd5+.} 19...Bxc4 20.Bd5+ {While this check wins an exchange for White, we will have enough compensation for it in the form of pawns and piece activity. We take the bishop.} 20...Nxd5 21.Rxb8+ {White wins the exchange.} 21...Kf7 {We've already taken a few pawns for it, and our light-squared bishop will be very strong and unopposed.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 {Blocking with the knight on c3 is also possible. And it's played half the time. We'll take on c3 and double the white pawns.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {They recapture. Our queen comes to e7 to put pressure on the weak e5-pawn.} 7...Qe7 8.Qd5 {White defends the attacked e5-pawn. It is impossible to add an attacker to it without giving up another pawn. This is completely fine, as getting active pieces is more important. We play in gambit style and push f6!} 8...f6 9.exf6 {White captures. Our knight returns to f6, attacking the white queen.} 9...Nxf6 10.Qd3 {The queen retreats, defending the two weak c-pawns. We'll prepare to get our bishop out.} 10...d6 11.g3 {White can continue with a fianchetto. Now castling is sensible.} 11...O-O 12.Bg2 {The bishop goes to g2. We'll double on the half-open e-file.} 12...Re8 13.O-O {White allows us to regain the pawn by capturing on e2. The queens are going to be exchanged. Let's see how this works out.} 13...Qxe2 14.Qxe2 {White recaptures. Our rook arrives on the seventh.} 14...Rxe2 15.Nd4 {And now White forces us to fix their structure. We have little choice but to take on d4.} 15...Nxd4 16.cxd4 {The pawn structure has been repaired, and White's pressure on the queenside is immense. Rather than defend passively, we offer our b7-pawn in order to get our remaining pieces out.} 16...Be6 17.Bxb7 {White grabs the pawn. We'll attack the bishop. The c4-pawn is also under fire.} 17...Rb8 18.Bf3 {Retreating while attacking our rook.} 18...Rc2 {But we can afford to leave our rook on an active square, attacking the queenside pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White blocks with a knight 49% of the time. We bring the queen out and attack the pawn once more.} 6...Qe7 7.a3 {White puts pressure on our bishop 43% of the time. Definitely a logical move. This allows us to set up a trap, however.} 7...Ngxe5 8.Nxe5 {White trades. We recapture, obviously.} 8...Nxe5 9.Bxe5 {Another trade? We need to save our bishop before recapturing.} 9...Bxd2+ 10.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures. And our queen does likewise.} 10...Qxe5 11.e3 {White is ready to develop their bishop. We'll prepare to fianchetto.} 11...b6 12.Rd1 {Putting pressure on d7. With that in mind, we push the d-pawn to d6 where it is supported by the c-pawn.} 12...d6 13.Qd5 {Proposing a trade. We'll move our rook and leave the onus on White to exchange queens.} 13...Rb8 14.Qxe5+ {White is in a simplifying mood! We recapture.} 14...dxe5 15.Be2 {White develops. Now there'll be no need to castle as we are close to the endgame. Black has more freedom to bring out the king.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 {Blocking with the knight on c3 is also possible. And it's played half the time. We'll take on c3 and double the white pawns.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {They recapture. Our queen comes to e7 to put pressure on the weak e5-pawn.} 7...Qe7 8.e3 {e3 seems less challenging. We simply regain the pawn.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Be2 {White defends the knight and develops the bishop. We support our knight as well.} 9...d6 10.Nd4 {Nd4 hopes for an exchange, but we won't do it. We'll just castle.} 10...O-O 11.O-O {White castles, too. The c-pawns are bad. We'll start to target them.} 11...Na5 12.Qa4 {White defends and attacks us. We'll simply support the knight with the b-pawn.} 12...b6 13.Bxe5 {Attempting to diminish the pressure. But when we take back with the pawn, White still has problems.} 13...dxe5 14.Nf3 {The knight goes back. We attack the queen.} 14...Bd7 15.Qc2 {The queen retreats to c2. Our rook enters the open file.} 15...Rad8 16.Rfd1 {White places their rook on the file, too.} 16...Bc6 {We place our bishop actively. The c-pawns are a permanent liability.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White blocks with a knight 49% of the time. We bring the queen out and attack the pawn once more.} 6...Qe7 7.e3 {White prepares to develop the light-squared bishop and castle.} 7...Ngxe5 8.Nxe5 {White trades.} 8...Nxe5 9.Be2 {White will castle.} 9...a5 10.O-O {Castling.} 10...d6 11.Nb3 {The idea is to control squares around the bishop.} 11...a4 12.a3 {a3 is played either way.} 12...Bc3 13.Qc2 {The queen prefers to capture, keeping the structure sound.} 13...Bxb2 14.Qxb2 {White takes our bishop.} 14...axb3 15.c5 {c5 to undermine our defense of e5.} 15...Be6 16.cxd6 {White trades.} 16...cxd6 17.Bxe5 {The bishop captures.} 17...dxe5 18.Bd1 {White really wants to take the pawn back.} 18...Bc4 *

