1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White blocks with a knight 49% of the time. We bring the queen out and attack the pawn once more.} 6...Qe7 7.e3 {White prepares to develop the light-squared bishop and castle.} 7...Ngxe5 8.Nxe5 {White trades.} 8...Nxe5 9.Be2 {White will castle.} 9...a5 10.O-O {Castling.} 10...d6 11.Nb3 {The idea is to control squares around the bishop.} 11...a4 12.Nd4 {The knight leaves.} 12...O-O 13.Nb5 {Now if Nb5...} 13...a3 14.bxa3 {White doubles their pawns.} 14...Bc5 {We just retreat. c6 is coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 12"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 {Blocking with the knight on c3 is also possible. And it's played half the time. We'll take on c3 and double the white pawns.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {They recapture. Our queen comes to e7 to put pressure on the weak e5-pawn.} 7...Qe7 8.e3 {e3 seems less challenging. We simply regain the pawn.} 8...Ngxe5 9.Nxe5 {White chooses to exchange knights. We recapture.} 9...Nxe5 10.Qd4 {Adding another attacker. We defend with the f-pawn.} 10...f6 11.c5 {White makes use of the pawns, trying to restrict our options for pawn play on the queenside. We'll develop our bishop on b6.} 11...b6 12.cxb6 {White captures, taking the opportunity to rid themselves of one of their weak c-pawns. We recapture, opening the a-file for our rook.} 12...axb6 13.a4 {White expands on the queenside. We fianchetto.} 13...Bb7 14.f3 {A natural move like f3, controlling the center, fails... White can't recapture as the knight would give a fork when capturing f3.} 14...Bxf3 15.Kf2 {White escapes the check, but...} 15...Bb7 {We just retreat, up a pawn with a better position.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nc3 {Blocking with the knight on c3 is also possible. And it's played half the time. We'll take on c3 and double the white pawns.} 6...Bxc3+ 7.bxc3 {They recapture. Our queen comes to e7 to put pressure on the weak e5-pawn.} 7...Qe7 8.Qd5 {White defends the attacked e5-pawn. It is impossible to add an attacker to it without giving up another pawn. This is completely fine, as getting active pieces is more important. We play in gambit style and push f6!} 8...f6 9.exf6 {White captures. Our knight returns to f6, attacking the white queen.} 9...Nxf6 10.Qd3 {The queen retreats, defending the two weak c-pawns. We'll prepare to get our bishop out.} 10...d6 11.g3 {White can continue with a fianchetto. Now castling is sensible.} 11...O-O 12.Bg2 {The bishop goes to g2. We'll double on the half-open e-file.} 12...Re8 13.Be3 {The bishop blocks our attack on the e2-pawn. But we can target the bishop with our knight!} 13...Ng4 14.Bg5 {Attacking our queen. We'll move the queen to an active location on f7, where it takes aim at both f2 and c4.} 14...Qf7 15.c5 {White tries to undermine our structure, but our pieces get active in the meantime! Let's hop into the center with the c6-knight!} 15...Nce5 16.Nxe5 {White exchanges a pair of knights, but leaves f2 to be taken.} 16...Qxf2+ 17.Kd2 {The king walks away.} 17...Nxe5 {We recapture. White's position has fallen apart. You should take care of business after this.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White blocks with a knight 49% of the time. We bring the queen out and attack the pawn once more.} 6...Qe7 7.a3 {White puts pressure on our bishop 43% of the time. Definitely a logical move. This allows us to set up a trap, however.} 7...Ngxe5 8.Nxe5 {White trades. We recapture, obviously.} 8...Nxe5 9.e3 {White defends against the checkmate threat. Our bishop was under attack. We trade.} 9...Bxd2+ 10.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures. d6 is a standard move in these positions.} 10...d6 11.Be2 {White prepares castling. We castle.} 11...O-O 12.O-O {And so does White. Our bishop is coming to b7.} 12...b6 13.b4 {White expands on the queenside.} 13...Bb7 {We fianchetto. So from here on you should analyze this position and the plans you could organize. Keep in mind you can go Nd7/a7-a5/Ra7/Rfa8, or if you're brave, you can play f5 and g5 and attack on the kingside!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White blocks with a knight 49% of the time. We bring the queen out and attack the pawn once more.} 6...Qe7 7.a3 {White puts pressure on our bishop 43% of the time. Definitely a logical move. This allows us to set up a trap, however.} 7...Ngxe5 8.Nxe5 {White trades. We recapture, obviously.} 8...Nxe5 9.e3 {White defends against the checkmate threat. Our bishop was under attack. We trade.} 9...Bxd2+ 10.Qxd2 {The queen recaptures. d6 is a standard move in these positions.} 10...d6 11.Be2 {White prepares castling. We castle.} 11...O-O 12.O-O {And so does White. Our bishop is coming to b7.} 12...b6 13.f3 {Perhaps an effort to make Bb7 less effective. We'll take steps to restrain the queenside expansion idea of b4, and prepare to attack the c4-pawn with Ba6.} 13...a5 14.b3 {White defends. We'll bring the bishop out.} 14...Ba6 15.Rac1 {White places themselves in a defensive position, keeping everything secured. We'll start to activate our rooks, placing the f8-rook on the half-open e-file and planning Ng6.} 15...Rfe8 16.Bg3 {That's why the bishop must move. The c4-pawn is now secure, so we'll bring our bishop to the long diagonal.} 16...Bb7 17.Rfe1 {The white rook steps to the e-file, too. We'll maneuver our knight to c5, in the process uncovering an attack on e3.} 17...Nd7 18.e4 {White pushes the weak pawn forward. Now we bring our knight to c5, attacking b3.} 18...Nc5 19.Qc3 {The queen defends.} 19...f6 {And we create a pawn wall of dark-squared pawns. We don't have a dark-squared bishop, so this is a sound strategy!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Budapest Gambit Main Lines / Study 2: White Plays 4.Bf4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/68a8fd6b-cb1a-4b18-9d2a-347484457123/chapters/58cb8823-f464-4515-9211-ad698dee58d8/studies/f66c0c6c-f1c6-4e9c-b94a-0117f58e5b66/lines"]

1.d4 {Let's look at important lines with 4.Bf4. What if White chooses to take their bishop out and defend the pawn? Well, it's another option. Hanging onto the pawn will only be possible for a couple of moves, since we'll play the moves we have prepared. As you'll see from the continuations, too, there are chances for us to develop an attack, even in the middle game.} 1...Nf6 2.c4 {Placing a second pawn in the center. We play the Budapest Gambit!} 2...e5 3.dxe5 {White accepts it. We jump forward and attack the pawn.} 3...Ng4 4.Bf4 {Now White defends it with the bishop. 4.Bf4 is opted for 20% of the time, equating to almost 780000 games! We add another attacker.} 4...Nc6 5.Nf3 {White brings another defender. And we check. The idea is to develop our bishop before playing Qe7.} 5...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 {White blocks with a knight 49% of the time. We bring the queen out and attack the pawn once more.} 6...Qe7 7.a3 {White puts pressure on our bishop 43% of the time. Definitely a logical move. This allows us to set up a trap, however.} 7...Ngxe5 8.axb4 {Grabbing on b4 is a huge blunder.} 8...Nd3# {That's a GG.} *